```ts
let a: string[] = ["hello", "world"];
let b: Array<string> = ["hello", "world"];
```
`T[]` is shorthand for the generic type `Array<T>`. The shorthand is more common, but the generic form makes it clear that `Array` is itself a generic object type like `Box<T>`.

```ts
type StringNumberPair = [string, number];

function doSomething(pair: [string, number]) {
  const a = pair[0]; // string
  const b = pair[1]; // number
}
doSomething(["hello", 42]); // OK
```
A `string[]` can have any length and only string elements; a tuple has a fixed length and per-position types. Accessing past the known length is a type error.

```ts
type Either2dOr3d = [number, number, number?]; // length 2 or 3

type StringNumberBooleans = [string, number, ...boolean[]];
type StringBooleansNumber = [string, ...boolean[], number];
```
Optional elements can only appear at the end and make the length a union (here `2 | 3`). Rest elements can sit at the start, middle, or end, and let a tuple describe "these fixed positions, plus any number of extras".

