How do you declare the type of `this` for a callback in TypeScript?
```ts
interface DB {
  filterUsers(filter: /* ? */): User[];
}
```

explanation:
```ts
const admins = db.filterUsers(function (this: User) {
  return this.admin;
});
```
Note the callback must be a `function` expression — arrow functions don't have their own `this`, so they can't satisfy a `this` parameter. TypeScript checks both that the implementation uses `this` consistently and that the caller invokes it with the right receiver.

```ts
function printId(id: number | string) {
  console.log(id.toUpperCase()); // Error — numbers have no toUpperCase
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    console.log(id.toUpperCase()); // OK — narrowed to string
  }
}
```
TypeScript only allows what is safe for all members; a `typeof` check narrows the union so member-specific operations become available.

question:
Why is the generic identity function better than one typed with `any`?
```ts
function identity(arg: any): any { return arg; }
function identity<Type>(arg: Type): Type { return arg; }
```

Why does this fail to compile?
```ts
function loggingIdentity<Type>(arg: Type): Type {
  console.log(arg.length); // error
  return arg;
}
```

explanation:
Someone could call `loggingIdentity(3)` — numbers have no `.length`. Two fixes: operate on arrays (`arg: Type[]`), or constrain the parameter:
```ts
interface Lengthwise { length: number; }
function loggingIdentity<Type extends Lengthwise>(arg: Type): Type {
  console.log(arg.length); // OK
  return arg;
}
```
After the constraint, calls must supply a value with a numeric `length`.

answer:
Put the type parameter list after the name:
```ts
interface GenericIdentityFn<Type> {
  (arg: Type): Type;
}

class GenericNumber<NumType> {
  zeroValue: NumType;
  add: (x: NumType, y: NumType) => NumType;
}
```

explanation:
Lifting the parameter to the interface/class level makes it visible to all members and forces users to pin it when using the type:
```ts
let myIdentity: GenericIdentityFn<number> = identity;
let n = new GenericNumber<number>();
```
Note generic classes are only generic over their *instance* side — static members can't use the class's type parameter.

