question:
What are `static` class members, and why can't they reference the class's type parameters?
```ts
class Box<Type> {
  static defaultValue: Type; // error — why?
}
```

explanation:
```ts
class MyClass {
  static x = 0;
  static printX() { console.log(MyClass.x); }
}
MyClass.printX();
```
If `static defaultValue: Type` were allowed, what type would `Box.defaultValue` have? There's no instance to pin `Type` down — which is exactly why the language forbids it.

If only a `get` exists, TypeScript infers the property as `readonly`. Conveniently, getters and setters may also disagree on types — a setter can accept a wider type than the getter returns:
```ts
class Thing {
  _size = 0;
  get size(): number { return this._size; }
  set size(value: string | number | boolean) {
    this._size = Number(value);
  }
}
```

